# Flutter/Dart testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' testing rules to `flutter_test` widget tests and `package:test` unit tests.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live under `test/`, mirroring `lib/`
  (`lib/order/order_service.dart` -> `test/order/order_service_test.dart`); no
  separate ad hoc fixture tree.
- A `testWidgets('<behavior it verifies>', ...)` description names observable
  behavior (`'shows an error banner when submit fails'`), not an implementation
  detail (`'calls setState'`).
- `group('OrderService', () { ... })` clusters cases for one class/widget,
  matching the project's existing suite.

## Widget tests

- Build the widget with `tester.pumpWidget(...)` wrapped in the ancestors it
  needs (`MaterialApp`/`CupertinoApp`, a `Localizations` scope, a
  state-management provider), matching the app's real tree.
- Locate with `find.byType`, `find.text`, `find.byKey`, `find.byIcon`; prefer
  `find.byKey` for a widget identified by role when its text/type could change
  without the behavior changing.
- `tester.pump()` advances one frame; `tester.pumpAndSettle()` waits for an
  animation, transition or async operation to finish. Not `pumpAndSettle()` by
  default: under a `Timer.periodic` or other endless animation it times out; use a
  bounded `pump(Duration(...))` there.
- Interact with `tester.tap`, `tester.enterText`, `tester.drag`, each followed by
  the `pump()`/`pumpAndSettle()` the interaction needs before asserting.

## Mocking the boundary, not the internals

- Mock at the external boundary (repository, HTTP client, platform-channel
  wrapper) defined as an abstract class, using `mocktail` (no code generation) or
  `mockito` (code generation).
- Never mock a class's own internal collaborator (a private helper, a widget's
  internal state).
- Verify a mocked interaction (`verify(() => mockRepository.fetchOrders()).called(1)`)
  only for calls that matter to the behavior; do not over-verify incidental calls.

## Async state in widget tests

- For state depending on a `Future`, `pump()` once for the loading state, then
  `pump()`/`pumpAndSettle()` after the mocked `Future` resolves, asserting the
  loading indicator is gone and the content is present.
- Never use `Future.delayed` or a real `Duration` wait in a test; control the
  mock's `Future` (a `Completer` the test completes, or an immediately resolving
  mock) and advance time with `pump()`/`pumpAndSettle()`/`tester.pump(duration)`.

## Fixtures and golden tests

- Fixtures (sample models, mock repository responses) live in `test/fixtures/` or
  a per-feature helper file, matching the project; no second fixture convention.
- A golden-image test (`matchesGoldenFile`) is opt-in: add one only where the
  project already has a golden setup and checked-in baselines, and regenerate
  with `flutter test --update-goldens` deliberately, not as a side effect.

## Running and coverage

- Run `flutter test` (`--coverage` when the project tracks coverage) before
  reporting a testing task done; fix a failing test by correcting the test or its
  fixture, not by weakening its `expect`/`find` assertion.
